'''WWJZ''' (640 [[AM broadcasting|AM]]) is a radio station [[city of license|licensed]] to [[Mount Holly, New Jersey]], serving [[Philadelphia]] and the [[Delaware Valley]]. The station airs [[Christian radio|catholic]]–[[talk radio]] programming and is owned and operated by [[Relevant Radio]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fcc.gov/fcc-bin/amq?call=WWJZ |title=WWJZ Facility Record |work=United States [[Federal Communications Commission]], audio division}}</ref>

The station's [[transmitter]] is located near the intersection of [[U.S. Route 206]] and [[County Route 530 (New Jersey)|CR-530]] in [[Pemberton Township, New Jersey]], and station offices are in [[Fort Washington, Pennsylvania]].<ref name="Radio Disney EEO">{{cite web |url=http://cdn.dolimg.com/en-US/radiodisney/media/EEO/EEO_Reports_9_24_14.pdf |title=Radio Disney EEO Reports |work=[[Radio Disney]] |access-date=August 9, 2014}}</ref> WWJZ operates with 50,000 watts in the daytime, the maximum permitted for AM stations by the [[Federal Communications Commission]]. But because 640&nbsp;kHz is a [[Clear-channel station|clear-channel frequency]], WWJZ must reduce power to 950 watts at night to avoid interfering with other radio stations such as [[KFI]] 640 in Los Angeles and [[CBN (AM)|CBN]] in [[St. John's, Newfoundland and Labrador]], Canada which are dominant Class A radio stations on [[640 AM]] in the United States.

==History==
WWJZ was owned by John Farina, the originator of the sound adopted by Al Ham's ''[[Music of Your Life]]'' [[adult standards]] [[radio format|format]]. Farina's dream was to re-establish the signal he had on 1460&nbsp;kHz in Mount Holly in the 1960s, as [[WJJZ (AM)|WJJZ]]. With the help of his long-time friend and engineer, Ted Schober, he got [[New Jersey]] its first 50-kilowatt AM radio station in many years and was able to put his beloved sound on the air again.

Until the inception of WWJZ operation in November 1992, there were no broadcast stations on 640&nbsp;kHz on the [[East Coast of the United States|East Coast]]. This was because [[KFI]] in Los Angeles is the clear-channel station on 640, in the days when Class I-A stations had few lesser stations on their frequencies, even thousands of miles away. When the F.C.C. relaxed those regulations, it allowed several new stations, including WWJZ, to go on the air on the 640 frequency.

The music of [[Brook Benton]], [[Tommy Dorsey]], [[Margaret Whiting]], [[Doris Day]], [[Frankie Laine]] and many others aired from October 1992 into 1993, emanating from an ancient [[General Electric]] transmitter of the type used by the venerable [[WABC (AM)|WJZ]] in its early days as the [[flagship (broadcasting)|flagship]] of the [[NBC]] [[Blue Network]]. Then a dispute between Farina and WWJZ's landlord, Edgar Cramer, put WWJZ off the air on July 9, 1993.

Not to be defeated, Farina re-established the station on a 1,700–watt temporary transmitter in [[Florence, New Jersey]], with the help of Nick Grand and Schober. The sound was well received, but the weaker signal did not compare to the big transmitter. Shortly thereafter, Farina had a stroke and died. Nick Grand continued the temporary operation as executor through the end of 1999, unable to make peace with Cramer until [[The Walt Disney Company]] made an offer to buy the station, to air its [[Radio Disney]] format in the Philadelphia [[Media market]]. After playing "[[Wish Me Luck as You Wave Me Goodbye]]" by [[Gracie Fields]] from the movie [[Shipyard Sally]], WWJZ began airing the Radio Disney format on September 12.<ref>"[https://www.americanradiohistory.com/Archive-M-Street/M-Street-Journal/M-Street-1999-09.pdf Format Changes & Updates]", ''The M-Street Journal''. Vol. 16 No. 37. September 15, 1999. p. 2. Retrieved January 7, 2025.</ref> The first song WWJZ played as Radio Disney was [[Elvis Presley]]'s cover of "[[Hound Dog (song)#Elvis Presley's version (1956)|Hound Dog]]". That marked the end of local feed on the signal.

[[File:radiodisneyphiladelphia.png|thumb|150px|Final Radio Disney logo for WWJZ]]

On August 13, 2014, Disney put WWJZ and 22 other Radio Disney stations up for sale, to focus on digital distribution of the Radio Disney network.<ref name=b&c-radiodisneysell2014>{{cite news|last1=Lafayette|first1=Jon|title=Exclusive: Radio Disney Moving Off Air to Digital|url=http://broadcastingcable.com/news/currency/exclusive-radio-disney-moving-air-digital/133166|access-date=August 13, 2014|date=August 13, 2014}}</ref><ref name=billboard-selling>{{cite magazine|title=Radio Disney to Sell the Majority of Its Stations|url=http://www.billboard.com/biz/articles/6214509/radio-disney-to-sell-the-majority-of-its-stations|magazine=Billboard|access-date=August 13, 2014|ref=August 13, 2014}}</ref> Disney originally planned to temporarily shut down the station on September 26.<ref name="Radio Insight">{{cite web |url=http://radioinsight.com/blog/headlines/89717/radio-disney-to-sell-all-but-one-station/ |title=Radio Disney To Sell All But One Station |date=August 13, 2014 |website=RadioInsight |access-date=August 13, 2014 |last=Venta |first=Lance}}</ref> However, WWJZ remained on the air and continued carrying Radio Disney programming until the sale closed.<ref name=nerw-rd>{{cite web|title=NERW Extra: No Signoffs for Disney AMs|url=http://www.fybush.com/nerw-20140926/|website=Northeast Radio Watch|date=September 26, 2014|access-date=September 27, 2014}} {{Subscription required}}</ref> On August 11, 2015, Relevant Radio's parent, the Starboard Media Foundation, agreed to purchase WWJZ for $3.5&nbsp;million.<ref name=fcc-saletoraycom>{{cite web|title=APPLICATION FOR CONSENT TO ASSIGNMENT OF BROADCAST STATION CONSTRUCTION PERMIT OR LICENSE|url=http://licensing.fcc.gov/cgi-bin/ws.exe/prod/cdbs/forms/prod/prefill_and_display.pl?Application_id=1683921&Service=AM&Form_id=314&Facility_id=43904|website=CDBS Public Access|publisher=[[Federal Communications Commission]]|access-date=August 12, 2015|date=August 12, 2015}}</ref> On September 18, WWJZ dropped the Radio Disney affiliation and went [[dark (broadcasting)|silent]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=NERW 20150921|url=https://www.fybush.com/nerw-20150921|website=Fybush|date=September 21, 2015|access-date=September 21, 2015}}</ref>
The station began broadcasting Catholic programming, 24 hours a day, when the acquisition was consummated on September 23.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.relevantradio.com/stories/relevant-radio-stati|title=Relevant Radio station coming to Philadelphia - Relevant Radio}}</ref> Radio Disney programming for the region later moved to the [[WXTU|WXTU-HD3]] digital subchannel, which has since ceased operations.

==Coverage==
WWJZ 640 can be heard by day in the [[Philadelphia metropolitan area]], the [[Jersey Shore]], and parts of [[Delaware]] and [[Maryland]]. The 1,000-watt nighttime signal is heard over Central New Jersey and Philadelphia's northern suburbs.

