'''Zoran Terzić''' ({{lang-sr-Cyrl|Зоран Терзић}}, born 9 July 1966) is a Serbian [[volleyball]] coach. He was the coach of the [[Serbia women's national volleyball team]] between 2002 and 2022, and under his leadership the team won 20 European, World and Olympic medals.<ref name=Danas>{{cite web|url=https://www.danas.rs/sport/zoran-terzic-nije-vise-selektor-odbojkasica-srbije/ |title=Zoran Terzić nije više selektor odbojkašica Srbije
|publisher=Danas |date=2022-01-17}}</ref> On club level he coaches [[Fenerbahçe Women's Volleyball|Fenerbahçe SK]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Team Roster – Serbia|url=http://www.cev.lu/Competition-Area/Competition.aspx?ID=701&PID=1335|work=cev.lu|access-date=3 October 2015}}</ref>

As a player, Terzić passed through all selections of the [[OK Crvena Zvezda|Red Star]] club in Belgrade between 1979 and 1986. He stopped playing in order to pursue his  studies. He graduated at the [[University of Belgrade]]'s [[Faculty of Sport and Physical Education]] in 1998.<ref name=fivb>{{cite web|url=http://www.fivb.org/EN/Volleyball/Competitions/Olympics/2008/W/Teams/Team_Roster.asp?TRN=WOG2008&TEAM=SRB |title=Coach Profile: Zoran Terzić |publisher=[[FIVB]] |access-date=2017-01-07}}</ref>

His first experience as a coach was as an assistant to Drago Nešić, head coach of the "IMT" Volleyball Club in Belgrade. In 1996, Terzić began working as a coach of the Red Star's junior team, with whom he won all available titles, from the championships of Belgrade to those of Serbia and [[FR Yugoslavia]]. At the suggestion of Aleksandar Boričić, head of the Red Star Volleyball Club, he started helping coach Red Star's women team, although it was not his original plan.<ref name=fivb/> Under Terzić's leadership, Red Star girls interrupted the 8-year winning streak of [[OK Jedinstvo Užice]] and won the 2001/02 and 2002/03 seasons of the [[Women's Volleyball League of Serbia|National League]].<ref name=fivb/><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.wienerliga.org/index.php?mod=tekst&id=1 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070323020453/http://www.wienerliga.org/index.php?mod=tekst&id=1 |url-status=usurped |archive-date=March 23, 2007 |title=Osvajači titula državnih prvaka 1945-2014 |trans-title=National Champions 1945-2014 |publisher=Wiener Städtische League |access-date=2017-02-07}}</ref>

Terzić took over the national team of FR Yugoslavia in 2002 (later Serbia and Montenegro and then Serbia), leading his teams to numerous successes, including a [[2018 FIVB Volleyball Women's World Championship|gold]] and [[2006 FIVB Volleyball Women's World Championship|bronze]] medal at the [[FIVB Volleyball Women's World Championship]], an Olympic silver at [[Volleyball at the 2016 Summer Olympics – Women's tournament|Rio 2016]] and bronze at [[Volleyball at the 2020 Summer Olympics – Women's tournament|Tokyo 2020]], the European Championship titles in [[2011 Women's European Volleyball Championship|2011]], [[2017 Women's European Volleyball Championship|2017]] and [[2019 Women's European Volleyball Championship|2019]], and a silver at the [[2015 FIVB Volleyball Women's World Cup|2015 World Cup]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://clubworldchampionships.2016.women.fivb.com/en/teams/vol-volero%20zrich|title=Overview - Volero Zürich - FIVB Volleyball Women's Club World Championship 2016 |website=clubworldchampionships.2016.women.fivb.com |access-date=2016-10-21}}</ref>

On 17 January 2022, Terzić announced that he will not coach the national team anymore, as his contract with the National Organization has expired and they mutually agreed not to renew it again.<ref name=Danas/>

